+PaperMono requires a running RTC with its voltage-loss flag cleared by setting
+valid time. Connect through the existing Wi-Fi setup or use Clock Sync first;
+the timer path does not invent a calendar value or clear that flag to enable
+itself. Intervals above 65,535 seconds round up to whole minutes. The first
+countdown period can be up to one second short, so this is not a precise
+wall-clock scheduler.
+
+The PaperMono route accepts a cold power-on only when the saved PMIC source
+is exclusively external GPIO wake, GPIO0 is the only enabled wake input, and
+the RTC reports an enabled timer interrupt with no clock-loss flag. It then
+cancels the countdown and wake input before starting the shared timer caller.
+Button, USB, software and brownout resets do not qualify. PMIC GPIO0 and the
+RTC interrupt output must not be shared with another wake owner.
+
+Power capture on PaperMono reads the PMIC's latched button event instead of
+installing an ESP GPIO interrupt. A failed read aborts autonomous work. The
+route checks again after the PMIC's command-settle delay and before shutdown.
+If shutdown returns, it cancels the timer and restarts normal reader startup;
+it never falls through to ESP-only sleep, which has no Power wake on this board.
+The remaining check-to-shutdown race needs a physical button test.
+
+The RTC route holds the PMIC LDO during shutdown, following M5Stack's RTC wake
+example. Its standby current has not been measured; figures from variants
+that release that rail do not apply. This proposal depends on the paired SDK
+RTC and PMIC changes and is not a released PaperMono capability.
